(A relational database stores related facts in tables made of rows and columns. A primary key) Tj T*
(uniquely identifies each row in its table and must not be null. A foreign key references a) Tj T*
(candidate key, commonly a primary key, in another table. The database can enforce) Tj T*
(referential integrity so that a reference cannot point to a row that does not exist. A) Tj T*
(foreign key does not have to use the same column name as the referenced key, but its values) Tj T*
(must be compatible.) Tj T*
(A one-to-many relationship means one row on the parent side can be related to many rows on) Tj T*
(the child side, while each child row refers to one parent. The foreign key is normally) Tj T*
(stored on the many side. For example, one conversation can have many messages, and each) Tj T*
(message can store the conversation's identifier. A many-to-many relationship allows rows on) Tj T*
(either side to relate to multiple rows on the other side. It is represented with a junction) Tj T*
(table containing foreign keys to both participating tables; a uniqueness constraint on the) Tj T*
(pair can prevent duplicate associations.) Tj T*
(An index is an auxiliary data structure that can help a database find rows without scanning) Tj T*
(every row in a table. An index can improve reads and joins when its columns match useful) Tj T*
(filters or ordering, but it consumes storage and must be maintained when rows are inserted,) Tj T*
(updated, or deleted. Indexes do not guarantee a faster query: the optimizer chooses a plan) Tj T*
(based on the query and available statistics. Frequently filtered ownership identifiers are) Tj T*
(common index candidates in a multi-user application.) Tj T*
(A database transaction groups operations into a unit that is committed or rolled back. ACID) Tj T*
(describes four transaction properties. Atomicity means all operations in the transaction) Tj T*
(take effect or none do. Consistency means a committed transaction preserves declared rules) Tj T*
(and constraints. Isolation controls how concurrent transactions observe one another's work.) Tj T*
(Durability means committed changes survive a failure according to the database's guarantees.) Tj T*
(ACID does not automatically include work in an external service such as a vector database;) Tj T*
(applications need retries, idempotency, or compensating cleanup for cross-system workflows.) Tj T*
(SQL joins combine rows from tables using a related condition. An INNER JOIN returns rows) Tj T*
(that satisfy the join condition on both sides. A LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left) Tj T*
(input and adds matching right-side values, using nulls when no right row matches. A join can) Tj T*
(combine messages with their conversation or a junction table with its documents. The join) Tj T*
(condition should use the intended key relationship; omitting it can create a Cartesian) Tj T*
(product with many unintended row combinations.) Tj T*
(Schema constraints and application authorization solve different problems. A foreign key can) Tj T*
(ensure that an association references existing rows, while an application query must still) Tj T*
(filter by the authenticated user's ownership. For example, a junction table may carry a user) Tj T*
(identifier and use composite foreign keys to ensure its conversation and document belong to) Tj T*
(that same user. The API should also validate ownership before creating the association, and) Tj T*
